From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 18:53:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 076/184] segacd: a command is only a command once it is on the
 wire

torn 0, badbcd 0, wrong 0-then-15, wait 0, seek 49, why 11: headers
stable, nothing torn, and not one read ever reached its sector. The
tearing theory dies by count, and the numbers convict a race instead.

Since Lunar taught this driver to pause between reads, the drive
hovers -- re-reading one track, streaming sectors continuously. The
withdraw guard ('once the decoder is producing, take the queued
command back so it is not re-sent forever') fires on the hover's very
next sector, inside 13 ms, faster than the exchange that would carry
the read command out. The drive never hears the read. It hovers
forever: forty-nine seeks, zero arrivals, every attempt a timeout.
And the hover's sectors, recorded into the ring before the command
posts, are the wrong-sector grabs.

The golden build -- the last disc that ever listed a file on C: --
dodged this by accident. Its seek-assist formula measured distance
from the disc's LENGTH rather than the head position (wrong, per the
runtime trace), so it fired a spurious command $A before every read,
which broke the hover and silenced the decoder before the read was
queued. The user's correlation, 'no file on C: since you removed $A',
was causal, argued from a CRT against the record, and correct.

The fix is the mechanism, not the accident: the level-4 handler stamps
when it actually posts a queued command; the withdraw guard and the
past-target test may only act on sectors decoded at least two
exchanges after that, and the ring is reset at the post so it speaks
only about the world after the drive was asked. Emulators never
showed any of it because their pause does not stream into the
decoder.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 45 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 5c516e5..1e31f0a 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -325,6 +325,12 @@ static UBYTE cdd_next[9];       /* built by the VBL, taken by level 4 */
 static ULONG cdd_outbuf[3];     /* aligned: the write below is long-wide */
 #define cdd_out ((UBYTE *)cdd_outbuf)
 static UBYTE cdd_handover;
+static volatile UWORD cdd_postmark;     /* cdd_ticks when the level-4
+                                         * handler last actually posted a
+                                         * queued command to the drive.
+                                         * "Queued" and "sent" are one
+                                         * exchange apart, and a hover
+                                         * fills that gap with sectors. */
 static UBYTE cdd_sent;          /* commands handed to the drive. Wraps, and
                                  * must: it used to saturate at 255, and 255
                                  * masked to three bits is a constant 7 -- so
@@ -732,6 +738,7 @@ void segacd_int_4_c(void)
         cdd_handover = 0;
         for (k = 0; k < 9; k++)
             cdd_queued[k] = cdd_next[k];
+        cdd_postmark = cdd_ticks;
     }
 
     cdd_command(cdd_queued);
@@ -1796,6 +1803,16 @@ static BOOL cd_fetch(ULONG lba)
             cd_quiet = 0;
             cd_streaming = 1;
             cd_seeks++;
+
+            /* Everything the decoder produces until the command is on
+             * the wire is the hover re-reading the old spot. Wait for
+             * the post -- one exchange, bounded by the budget -- and
+             * discard it, so the ring and the past-target test speak
+             * only about the world after the drive was asked. */
+            while (cdd_handover && cd_budget > 0 && cd_time_left())
+                cd_budget--;
+            cd_ring_reset();
+            cdc_ints = 0;
         } else {
             cd_hits++;
         }
@@ -1857,11 +1874,29 @@ static BOOL cd_fetch(ULONG lba)
                 }
             }
 
-            if (cdc_ints) {
-                /* Sectors are arriving, so the drive has done as it was
-                 * told. Stop telling it: a queued command goes out on
-                 * every exchange, so leaving the seek in the queue asks
-                 * the drive to start over before it ever arrives. */
+            if (cdc_ints && !cdd_handover
+                    && (UWORD)(cdd_ticks - cdd_postmark) >= 2) {
+                /* Sectors are arriving AND the read has actually been
+                 * on the wire for two exchanges. Both conditions,
+                 * because since the drive pauses between reads it
+                 * hovers -- re-reading one track, streaming sectors
+                 * continuously -- so "the decoder is producing" says
+                 * nothing about OUR command having been heard. On
+                 * hardware the hover's next sector lands inside 13 ms,
+                 * faster than the exchange that would carry the
+                 * command out, and withdrawing on it replaced the read
+                 * with idle before the drive ever heard it: forty-nine
+                 * seeks, zero arrivals, every attempt a timeout. The
+                 * golden build dodged this by accident -- its
+                 * disc-length seek formula fired a spurious command $A
+                 * first, which broke the hover and silenced the
+                 * decoder before the read was queued. The user's
+                 * correlation ("no file on C: since you removed $A")
+                 * was causal, and this is the mechanism.
+                 *
+                 * Stop telling it: a queued command goes out on every
+                 * exchange, so leaving the seek in the queue asks the
+                 * drive to start over before it ever arrives. */
                 if (!cd_quiet) {
                     cd_quiet = 1;
                     cdd_ask_idle();
